<p>At 2am on a Tuesday, your dog starts vomiting repeatedly and won't stop. By 3am you're at the emergency vet. By 4am you're looking at a bill estimate on a clipboard.</p>

<p><strong>$2,400. Due before treatment begins.</strong></p>

<p>This happens to pet owners every single day. Most of them had no idea this moment was coming. Most of them had no fund ready for it.</p>

<p>This article gives you the exact number you need — by pet type, size, and breed — so that clipboard moment never catches you unprepared.</p>

<h2>Why a Pet Emergency Fund Is Not Optional</h2>

<p>Pet insurance is valuable. But it doesn't solve the immediate problem — emergency vets require payment at the time of service, before your insurance claim is processed.</p>

<p>Even with insurance, you need cash available on day one. Your reimbursement comes later. The vet bill comes now.</p>

<p>An emergency fund is your first line of defense. Insurance is your second.</p>

<h2>What Pet Emergencies Actually Cost</h2>

<p>These are real 2025 emergency vet cost ranges — not estimates, not best-case scenarios.</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Emergency</th><th>Average Cost</th></tr>
<tr><td>Intestinal obstruction (swallowed object)</td><td>$1,500 - $6,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broken bone</td><td>$1,500 - $4,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cruciate ligament tear</td><td>$3,500 - $5,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bloat / GDV (stomach rotation)</td><td>$3,000 - $8,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Toxin ingestion / poisoning</td><td>$500 - $3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Urinary blockage (cats)</td><td>$750 - $3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Respiratory distress</td><td>$500 - $2,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bite wound / attack</td><td>$500 - $3,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hit by car</td><td>$2,000 - $10,000+</td></tr>
<tr><td>Seizure evaluation</td><td>$500 - $2,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>BOAS surgery (French Bulldogs)</td><td>$2,000 - $6,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Back surgery / IVDD (Dachshunds)</td><td>$4,000 - $8,000</td></tr>
</table>

<blockquote>The median emergency vet visit in 2025 costs $1,500-$2,000. That is the number you need available at minimum — at all times.</blockquote>

<h2>How Much You Need by Pet Type</h2>

<h3>Dogs — Small (under 25 lbs)</h3>
<p><strong>Minimum emergency fund: $2,000</strong><br/>
<strong>Recommended emergency fund: $3,000</strong></p>
<p>Small dogs face real emergencies but surgeries cost less than large dogs. Key risks: dental disease, IVDD in Dachshunds, foreign body ingestion, luxating patella.</p>

<h3>Dogs — Medium (25-60 lbs)</h3>
<p><strong>Minimum emergency fund: $3,000</strong><br/>
<strong>Recommended emergency fund: $5,000</strong></p>
<p>Medium dogs have the widest range of emergency risks. Labs and Goldens are notorious for eating foreign objects — $3,000-$6,000 to remove. Cruciate tears are common and expensive.</p>

<h3>Dogs — Large (over 60 lbs)</h3>
<p><strong>Minimum emergency fund: $5,000</strong><br/>
<strong>Recommended emergency fund: $7,000</strong></p>
<p>Large dogs cost more for every procedure — more anesthesia, more medication, longer recovery. Bloat is a life-threatening risk in deep-chested large breeds.</p>

<h3>Cats</h3>
<p><strong>Minimum emergency fund: $2,000</strong><br/>
<strong>Recommended emergency fund: $3,000</strong></p>
<p>Male cats face a specific high-cost emergency: urinary blockage. Left untreated it becomes fatal. Treatment costs $750-$3,000. Every male cat owner should have this fund ready.</p>

<h3>Rabbits</h3>
<p><strong>Minimum emergency fund: $1,500</strong><br/>
<strong>Recommended emergency fund: $2,500</strong></p>
<p>GI stasis is the most common rabbit emergency — when their digestive system stops moving. It's fatal within 24-48 hours and costs $500-$2,000 to treat.</p>

