<h2>The 3-Fund System for Pet Owners</h2>

<h3>Fund 1: Immediate emergency fund</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Amount:</strong> $2,000-$8,000 depending on breed</li>
<li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Available within 24 hours for emergency vet payment</li>
<li><strong>Where:</strong> High-yield savings account, separate from regular savings</li>
<li><strong>Rule:</strong> Never spend it on anything except vet bills</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fund 2: Annual vet fund</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Amount:</strong> $500-$1,500/year contributed monthly</li>
<li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Routine care, checkups, dental cleanings, preventive medications</li>
<li><strong>Where:</strong> Same HYSA or dedicated checking account</li>
<li><strong>Rule:</strong> Replenish after each use</li>
</ul>

<h3>Fund 3: Senior pet fund</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Amount:</strong> Start contributing at age 6 for dogs, age 10 for cats</li>
<li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Increased vet costs of aging pets — arthritis, cancer screening, mobility support</li>
<li><strong>Where:</strong> Separate savings or investment account</li>
<li><strong>Rule:</strong> Start before you need it, not after</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to Do If You Don't Have the Fund Yet</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Open a separate savings account today. Name it "Pet Emergency Fund." Put $100 in it to start.</li>
<li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Set up automatic transfers — $100-$200/month on payday.</li>
<li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Get pet insurance immediately while you build the fund.</li>
<li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Apply for CareCredit before you need it — a medical credit card accepted at most emergency vets.</li>
<li><strong>Step 5:</strong> Set a 6-month goal. Six months of automatic transfers gets most pet owners to their minimum target.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Uncomfortable Truth</h2>

<p>The average American household has less than $1,000 in savings for unexpected expenses. The average emergency vet bill is $1,500-$2,000. That gap is where pet owners end up making devastating decisions — not because they don't love their pet, but because they didn't see this coming.</p>

<blockquote>The emergency fund is not a luxury. It is part of the cost of responsible pet ownership.</blockquote>

<p>Your pet cannot advocate for themselves in a crisis. You have to.</p>

